1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,300
I'm Yoko Hikasa. I'm Ayane Sakura. Here we are for episode two.

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:12,160
So this is how the show starts every time, not just the first episode. Did you listen to our first broadcast?

3
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:18,780
I did, I did. No, she's lying! That lie came out awfully fast! It was funny, wasn't it?

4
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:24,160
We just found out that you didn't listen, Yoko Hikasa. You didn't catch the first broadcast live.

5
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:30,780
Chanko, our show writer, and I were commenting on it on LINE as it aired.

6
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:38,520
Well, near the end, about 25 minutes in. Hiyo was in that conversation too. Yes, I was.

7
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:49,180
We were saying, “Oh, so they add echoes and sound effects.” And that Yama-chan would play them live at the next recording.

8
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:59,700
Live, when there's a chance to add them. Since we were talking about that, I assumed Hiyo was listening too.

9
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:11,180
Then she came in today and said, “I only heard the first three minutes.” What do you mean? You can listen to the first three minutes free.

10
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:21,380
She hadn't installed radiko. She only listened to the three-minute preview available to people who haven't downloaded it.

11
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:27,900
Then she gave up, decided she'd heard it, and went, “That was hilarious!” Praising herself.

12
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:39,080
Chanko and I actually listened. How was it? Taxi drivers would probably change the station. They would.

13
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:48,840
Were we too loud? Did you hear what's on at the same time? Gen Hoshino's All Night Nippon, and Suzaki Nishi on A&G. Nobody's going to listen to us in that slot.

14
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:56,140
And Bakusho Mondai. So that's why this slot was available! Nobody wanted it.

15
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:03,300
This slot... Don't call it a death slot! Why put us there?

16
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:13,760
You thought we could beat them? Not a chance, you idiot! It's Gen Hoshino! The guy who sang “Koi”!

17
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:27,520
Gen Hoshino these days... What is that supposed to mean? He's amazing. He even invites voice actors onto his show.

18
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:39,800
We'll never get invited. We're talking about Gen Hoshino while his show is on! People are switching stations right this second.

19
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:47,860
Then Gen Hoshino needs to talk about our radio show too, or it isn't fair. That's a bold demand! I never said any of that.

20
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:52,980
You suddenly dropped that on me! Don't throw me under the bus like that.

21
00:02:52,980 --> 00:03:00,460
Well, I'm... I'm on Gen Hoshino's side, sorry. On his side? Our birthdays are only a day apart.

22
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:07,220
That has nothing to do with it! They're different days! We're practically twins. No, you're not! Different days!

23
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:21,200
Our show's official X account, formerly Twitter... They're not called tweets anymore. Posts? The posts have so many emojis.

24
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:33,340
The post right after the broadcast was full of hearts, like a high school girl's account. We said it looked like a teenage girl's Twitter.

25
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:44,680
We were like, “Is Iguchi-san writing this? That's creepy!” I was honestly about to lose it. “What is this?”

26
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:54,020
All three of us were furious. Sorry, not Chanko. Hiyo and I were going, “Iguchi-san, this tweet is so creepy!”

27
00:03:54,020 --> 00:04:05,020
Chanko explained, “It's written by a female assistant writer who wasn't at the first recording. It's fine.”

28
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:20,860
We met her for the first time today. She's our “high school girl.” That's her nickname. The show's resident high school girl.

29
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:31,860
Nice to meet you, “high school girl.” Is that a pun on “female show writer”? She's our high school girl. She's 29.

30
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:43,920
Is that why you keep saying it? She's 29? She's getting up there! Stop that! That one hurts me too.

31
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:50,760
That came right back at you! Hiyo, you're hurting yourself with that one. Be careful.

32
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:59,420
Our “high school girl” staff member writes the posts, so picture a high school girl when you send your replies.

33
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:05,120
Didn't you do that prank on Kito? We didn't. Come to think of it, no.

34
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:14,080
I was going to meet Akari after the last recording. We talked about it in episode one.

35
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:22,980
We were talking about how Yoko Hikasa seems emotionally unstable on LINE. Listen to episode one for the details.

36
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:35,920
Since I was having dinner with Kito, we planned a prank: “Message me on LINE, and I'll reply formally again.” We wanted to see how Akari would react. Like, “Thank you very much.”

37
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:43,480
That sounds awful now. Poor Akari. So you never got a message? That's right.

38
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:53,700
I forgot. We did meet, though. Don't forget in a single day, Hiyo! Don't forget she exists!

39
00:05:53,700 --> 00:06:03,880
Next time! Except if she listens to this, she'll know. It's over. The plan's fallen apart.

40
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:10,600
Enjoy the awkward relationship between Akari Kito and Yoko Hikasa. Thank you very much.

41
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:19,060
Hey, Saku, can I tell you something funny that happened today? Sounds good. What is it?

42
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:26,840
I was eating gapao rice. Thai food. Gapao rice! Is that really so surprising?

43
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:42,380
The restaurant had a huge lunch menu, and the set even came with salad and oolong tea. Oolong tea!

44
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:57,380
Don't put an echo on “oolong tea”! Don't turn that into the joke! I still can't tell where the punchline of your stories is, so I react to everything.

45
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:00,380
I haven't gotten there yet! It's only episode two. I'm about halfway through the story.

46
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:09,940
Even with an extra serving of cilantro, it was only a little over 1,000 yen. I was impressed, then looked up as I was eating.

47
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:16,060
There was a painting of a naked man on the wall.

48
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:23,980
And that part was drawn too. Don't call it “that part”! Just because we're on at one in the morning!

49
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:36,560
I was going, “Whoa, that part!” Don't say you were using that as your side dish.

50
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:42,980
There were other questionable things, though. Here's the photo.

51
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:55,080
Oh, you're right. Looks delicious, doesn't it? It does. The gapao rice, I mean.

52
00:07:55,080 --> 00:08:05,620
You startled me! “Looks delicious”—the gapao rice, I mean! That's inverted word order. You really left that clarification until the end.

53
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:15,620
When I pinched the photo, I was zooming in on the gapao rice. That's what I meant. That was very “Tochi Hiroki.”

54
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:25,620
That only makes sense if you know voice actors' names. Hiroki Tochi, right? That's funny.

55
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:40,120
Speaking of that, you know that place near a certain recording studio? Where? What place? How do I describe it?

56
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:49,120
The gapao place is nearby, right here. Here? Yes. The same restaurant? That's right.

57
00:08:49,120 --> 00:09:00,120
That restaurant? The Thai place near here, right? I was just about to talk about that place too.

58
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:10,120
It's so good, right? It's delicious! The gapao rice, I mean.

59
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:22,120
Their acai bowls are good too. They have those? They do. I've only been at lunch, but I often go early to eat because I want their acai bowl.

60
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:33,000
They have a huge painting covering a wall. And what's in that painting...

61
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:45,600
It's a little scary when you look closely. My memory is vague, but it says something in Japanese like, “Mom, I'm sorry I was born.”

62
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:49,300
It's really creepy. I want you to see it.

63
00:09:49,300 --> 00:10:00,280
That's quite a painting! Let's go together next time; I'll show you. I didn't know about your painting. I always think of the creepy one.

64
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:08,740
Let's all go together. Let's do that. Apparently the second floor is a hangout for those “Minato girls.”

65
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:22,440
Are we talking about the same restaurant? Something's off. Are these different places? We'll compare notes later. The details aren't quite matching up.

66
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:31,200
That surprised me. That was fun. Shall we finally start? Okay, here we go. Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions.

67
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:50,160
This show... Hang on, my voice is all dried out.

68
00:10:50,160 --> 00:11:00,160
This is a talk show where two women who could chat for hours if left alone allow no digressions and talk for exactly 30 minutes. Don't talk over me!

69
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:07,160
The show also has an official X account. Please follow it and join the conversation with the hashtag “余談.”

70
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:18,200
I thought that title announcement sounded familiar—it's like After 6 Junction, isn't it? Really? I love TBS Radio. No, definitely not.

71
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:24,960
We didn't think that deeply about it. Oh, really? Drinking this water just reminded me of something.

72
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:36,600
When we got here, there was water that our “high school girl” bought us. I was thirsty and went to drink it, but it was already open.

73
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:46,200
Hiyo, are you going to tell that story? We said it might be tricky to discuss. What, we're not? After everything else we've said, we can't tell this one?

74
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:52,280
Fair point. Let's tell it before we get a sponsor! So it was open? Right.

75
00:11:52,280 --> 00:12:01,480
I thought our staff member had opened it for me, but Saku's bottle wasn't open. A strange experience.

76
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:07,800
Something might have been mixed into it. What are you planning to do after getting me drunk?

77
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:23,340
The picture quality is crap; has it been improved? Why does this start with insults? This is a show where you hear our planning meeting. You picked the wrong people. And the wrong music.

78
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:32,600
Thirty-eight! Stop it! No, I'm 24. We're talking about Akari Kito now. She's the type whose personality changes a little on LINE.

79
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:42,680
Jungle... [unclear]. You were my enemy all along! That's BL. Hey, that's boys' love! Stop it.

80
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:49,500
Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions. Once again, I'm Yoko Hikasa. And I'm Ayane Sakura.

81
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:58,540
So the jingle is made from highlights of the last episode. Wow, I didn't expect that. That's awful! What terrible highlights!

82
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:08,300
Those remarks only work in context! This is what happens with media clips... You're the media doing it! The dark side of the media.

83
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:19,120
Darkness incarnate. What does that mean? Don't you mean “evil incarnate”? Darkness personified. Yama-chan.

84
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:28,880
Something occurred to me when I listened to those three minutes. Doesn't my laugh sound gross?

85
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:40,780
You've done radio for over ten years, and you're saying that now? It's because it's your own voice. I find my own voice and laugh weird too.

86
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:49,540
You can't help that. Then let's carry on. Do you think my laugh sounds gross? Never. And I've never thought that about your voice either, Hiyo.

87
00:13:49,540 --> 00:13:57,080
Love you, bestie. Mail! What was that just now?

88
00:13:57,080 --> 00:14:02,620
The “Mail!” thing when you read messages. Don't you know it? Mail! Surely you know it?

89
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:06,320
You don't know it? Wait, what? Hang on.

90
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:14,740
You're the older one, and you're teasing me about the generation gap between us? Wait a minute. Yes, exactly.

91
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:22,300
It's the talking mailbox on Downtown DX. It says “Mail!” and a message pops out of the mailbox.

92
00:14:22,300 --> 00:14:30,560
Sorry. This is from Samasama; thank you.

93
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:38,780
“Hikasa, who's stuck in the era of ‘was’ and ‘now,’ and Sakura, whose background always seems to be roses...”

94
00:14:38,780 --> 00:14:46,080
Hey, has anyone changed my Wikipedia photo? Let's see. Good evening.

95
00:14:46,080 --> 00:15:02,000
“You talked about outdated slang. Are you still saying ‘mekkawa’? These days people express cuteness with ‘kawachii’ and ‘teetee.’”

96
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:14,820
We got lots of messages after discussing outdated slang last week. Bad news: the photo hasn't changed. Hey, I'm serious about this!

97
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:26,080
Somebody! Am I really this unpopular? Isn't there an Ayane Sakura fan brigade or something? There must be!

98
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:36,740
What are my 170,000 Instagram followers doing? Go! Do it! Go, Pikachu! Get it done by next week!

99
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:47,540
That's right. Hit Wikipedia with 200,000 volts! Bzzzz! Oh, you're actually doing it.

100
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:57,720
That's so low! Pikachu, when did your voice get that deep? Bring back Ikue's Pikachu!

101
00:15:57,720 --> 00:16:09,320
Pikasha! Hiyo, come back! That's not Pikachu; it's Pikasha. Thunderbolt! Pikasha!

102
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:17,240
Should we really be this pleased with something like that? This is getting ridiculous. It really is.

103
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:26,360
One more, from Odayaka na Otaku: “I listened to your new show right away. It feels like a breath of fresh air.”

104
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:38,340
“But you seem out of touch with youth trends. I snorted at the ‘was’ and ‘now’ bit.” That's annoying! “Are you still using ‘(laugh)’?”

105
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:49,960
Why? These days it's “kusa,” meaning “grass.” You mean “w”? No, the actual kanji for grass. Not “w”?

106
00:16:50,540 --> 00:16:57,940
Like, “I love how Hikasa talks, kusa.” You actually say “kusa” out loud? Yes. That's how it is these days.

107
00:16:57,940 --> 00:17:02,860
And “kawachii” and “teetee.” What's “kawachii”? Chiikawa? No.

108
00:17:03,960 --> 00:17:13,140
It means “cute.” Why not just say “kawaii”? No, it has to be “kawachii.” “Teetee” means “precious,” right? I know that much.

109
00:17:13,540 --> 00:17:24,240
One more. This show really is just reading messages, huh? From Rin: “I'm in my second year of high school, and this is my first-ever message to a radio show.” An actual high school girl!

110
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:31,940
Our 29-year-old “high school girl”... Aren't they going to fight? Don't make them fight! Stay in the ring! Stay in the ring!

111
00:17:32,340 --> 00:17:40,160
“Is ‘now’ outdated slang? I'm a high school student and use it all the time, so that really got me down.”

112
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:47,440
So it's come around about five times, and I'm actually the high school girl here? No.

113
00:17:47,440 --> 00:18:01,640
“Is Satoru Gojo the ‘emperor’ for teenagers?” Who? The Jujutsu Kaisen character. What do you mean, emperor?

114
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:12,800
Like Toshiyuki Morikawa? Oh, that's what you mean. In certain circles, maybe.

115
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:19,960
In summer and winter, he might be a bit of an emperor.

116
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:32,120
Who's his partner? Geto? From Marusu. Hikasa, do you have the makings of a BL fangirl? No.

117
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:44,520
“Hello, Hikasa and Sakura.” Good evening. So stubborn! No flexibility. Good evening!

118
00:18:44,520 --> 00:18:56,140
“I listened to episode one. I laughed at how Toyama gets left behind by the speed of your conversation, and how Kito suspects Hikasa has a different personality on LINE.”

119
00:18:56,140 --> 00:19:03,900
“I'm 19, but the Death Note reference really landed for me. I'll keep supporting the show.”

120
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:12,680
Why Death Note all of a sudden? We said something like, “That's Death Note,” didn't we? Was it about whether teenagers would get it?

121
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:20,740
Death Note would still land. It's a masterpiece. A timeless classic. It's still great even if you read it now.

122
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:29,560
What was it? Something like, “I've won.” Is that Yue? Not Yue—Light. Yue is from Cardcaptor Sakura.

123
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:37,000
You just gave away your generation. Yukito, right.

124
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:46,800
Apparently Nao listened to our first episode last week. Really? How do you know? Did she say so? On her own radio show.

125
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:56,080
Do you write for Nao Toyama's show too? Oh, the staff told you. But you do work on Tomori's show, right?

126
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:11,180
Tomori really loves you, Saku, and apparently wants to get closer to you. That's lovely to hear. It really is.

127
00:20:11,180 --> 00:20:21,180
I'm scared of interacting with juniors. I don't want even a little interaction to turn into accusations of bullying or sexual harassment.

128
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:33,880
Bring it on! You're fearless. Hikasa is really good with juniors. She can get along with absolutely everyone.

129
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:42,320
That's not true. This is where Hikasa and I will never see eye to eye. She's the type to be friends with the whole human race.

130
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:49,420
No, I'm not. Is there anyone you dislike? Of course! No, there isn't! I hardly ever hear you talk about people you dislike.

131
00:20:49,420 --> 00:21:01,340
Then do you think I really like Iguchi-san? Why did you just hurt Producer Iguchi for no reason? That's scary!

132
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:08,180
It's only episode two; I haven't found someone to tease yet. But as a target for teasing...

133
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:21,440
You can only come up with Iguchi because you're the kind of person who can't immediately think of anyone you dislike. People I dislike come to mind first. That's impressive in its own way.

134
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:31,780
Then maybe you actually like me quite a bit? I wouldn't do a radio show with you if I hated you!

135
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:45,860
Listen, “chuki-chuki lovely-chan”! See? That's my knowledge of current trends. I'm with the times, everyone!

136
00:21:45,860 --> 00:21:55,480
“Chiki-chiki bon”? What's that? No “chiki-chiki bon”—that really gives away your age.

137
00:21:55,480 --> 00:22:04,140
And me, with Saku... We'll keep talking even if you signal us. Giving us the “wrap it up” gesture won't help.

138
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:14,880
Since around the first recording, I've been telling lots of people, “I'm doing a radio show with Saku.”

139
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:19,340
You're acting a little smug. Why? Your chin's sticking out.

140
00:22:19,340 --> 00:22:30,840
I was acting like the senior, going, “Well, she's a handful, that junior of mine.”

141
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:35,620
I was saying things like, “Yeah, we're doing it together.”

142
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:45,660
I thought it might be bad if that eventually got back to Saku. So I considered that first before telling people... [unclear].

143
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:51,180
This is so fun. I'm happy to hear that. I was the one who approached you, after all.

144
00:22:51,180 --> 00:23:00,720
Actually, just before the first recording, I got a bit of the “new-show blues.” What do you mean?

145
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:09,520
I was the one who asked, “Hiyo, would you do a radio show with me? Would you team up with me?” And you were a little nervous then.

146
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:20,260
Well, a little, you know... What is that? What are you doing? You were a bit nervous, weren't you?

147
00:23:20,260 --> 00:23:32,800
Because I've mostly only done radio shows tied to anime and things. Go on. You're such a pain! What's with this woman?

148
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:36,360
Who was that woman just now?

149
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:47,860
But I like this side of Hikasa, and this was the kind of radio show I wanted, so I asked her. If I join in too, though, it'll get out of hand.

150
00:23:47,860 --> 00:24:01,360
At first Hiyo said, “Maybe our relationship works because of the distance we have now. We don't know what will happen if we get too close.”

151
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:11,820
What? If we get too close... I wasn't rejecting you! She was saying she had some worries.

152
00:24:11,820 --> 00:24:25,820
Because I like Saku. I like you too, Hiyo, so I understand that worry. But we've been good friends for about 14 years, haven't we? I told her we'd be fine.

153
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:37,340
I said, “Once the show starts, let's not go out to eat too often.” But let's go! I'd be lonely. We'd run out of things to talk about on the show. That's true.

154
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:50,320
Then she said, “Well, in that case.” I confidently talked Hiyo into it, but about two weeks before we started, I began wondering if it was really the right choice.

155
00:24:50,320 --> 00:25:05,560
I'd pushed so hard to persuade her, but I didn't want to lose what we'd built together over the years. I started worrying about that.

156
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:16,140
I wondered if I'd made the wrong choice. I even started dreading the first episode a little.

157
00:25:16,140 --> 00:25:28,440
I was worrying about how we could keep our relationship the way it was and still make the radio show work.

158
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:36,880
But shortly before episode one, we saw each other at a voice recording, remember? We did.

159
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:46,460
Hiyo burst through the studio door going, “Yay! Good morning, everyone!”

160
00:25:46,460 --> 00:25:53,580
And I thought, with someone like her, we'll probably be fine.

161
00:25:53,580 --> 00:26:07,820
Even today, for episode two, she came in by flinging the door open: “Good morning, everyone!” With a big “Ta-da!”

162
00:26:07,820 --> 00:26:14,500
I thought, with her, we could probably keep this going forever. That's hilarious.

163
00:26:23,580 --> 00:26:33,680
You've been listening to Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions. It's nearly time to wrap up.

164
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:46,140
We welcome messages from our listeners. For postcards, the postal code is 150... No, it's 105! Don't get it wrong.

165
00:26:46,140 --> 00:26:52,660
105-8002. It's been ten years since Hikasa last had a regular show of her own on Bunka Hoso.

166
00:26:52,660 --> 00:27:02,100
Address it to Bunka Hoso, Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions. Remember I said I'd lost my access pass?

167
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:08,680
I couldn't get in. Then Iguchi contacted me asking if I still had it.

168
00:27:08,680 --> 00:27:21,200
I said I'd look for it. Around the day before yesterday, he asked again, “Did you find it?” I looked again, but it really wasn't there.

169
00:27:21,200 --> 00:27:31,940
For email, use all lowercase: yodan@joqr.net. That's yodan@joqr.net. “Yodan” is spelled Y-O-D-A-N.

170
00:27:31,940 --> 00:27:44,840
The show airs on Bunka Hoso every Tuesday at 25:00—that is, Wednesday at 1 a.m. The video version follows on Thursday at 9:30 p.m.

171
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:53,780
Audio-only archives are also available on the internet radio station Onsen every Thursday at 10 p.m. Probably the shredder...

172
00:27:53,780 --> 00:28:01,640
Then that's good. We also have a YouTube channel.

173
00:28:01,640 --> 00:28:11,780
Membership includes video archives and after-talks. Episode two is probably free too. Even the after-talk! Please subscribe to the channel.

174
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:24,680
Episode two is free too? We've already passed 1,000 subscribers, so it says we might be able to offer membership from this episode. I don't really understand how it works.

175
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:32,200
Total views... total... I don't really understand. Then you can set up membership? That's right.

176
00:28:32,200 --> 00:28:43,200
Changing the subject: I'm not wearing makeup today. Already? That surprised me. That's all.

177
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:52,080
That's all for this episode. Your hosts were Yoko Hikasa and... Grammar! Ayane Sakura. Bye-bye!

178
00:28:52,080 --> 00:29:03,600
Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions: After-Talk. I'm Yoko Hikasa. And I'm Ayane Sakura.

179
00:29:03,600 --> 00:29:10,400
The show airs on Bunka Hoso every Tuesday at 25:00, or Wednesday at 1 a.m. The video version follows on Thursday at 9:30 p.m.

180
00:29:10,400 --> 00:29:18,320
Audio-only archives are also available on Onsen every Thursday at 10 p.m. This is the show's YouTube-exclusive after-talk.

181
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:28,760
Aren't there fewer staff already, from episode two? It's just that my agency's manager isn't coming anymore. So there's nobody keeping an eye on us!

182
00:29:28,760 --> 00:29:38,140
The Onsen representative isn't here today either. They're not feeling well. Oh, I see. Aki isn't here, come to think of it.

183
00:29:38,140 --> 00:29:45,680
Shouldn't we use nicknames? Everyone has one. Chanko and “high school girl” are both nicknames. “Iguchi” is a nickname too.

184
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:55,880
Iguchi used to have the nickname “Satetsu.” Iron sand? That kind of satetsu? Yes, iron sand.

185
00:29:55,880 --> 00:30:04,400
I thought you meant “satetsu” as in Seishun no Satetsu—setbacks of youth. Or like the writer Satetsu Takeda. Then who is he supposed to be?

186
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:13,700
You mean that stuff you can collect from sand? Yosuke Iguchi. Yosuke Iguchi.

187
00:30:13,700 --> 00:30:25,860
He doesn't really give off an “iron sand” vibe. He'll always be Satetsu to me. His hair did look a bit like iron sand back in the Dot-i days.

188
00:30:25,860 --> 00:30:41,000
But “Iguchi” isn't very interesting. “Satetsu” feels a little... I just held back what I was about to say.

189
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:55,440
It's YouTube, so isn't it fine? Doesn't it sound too intellectual? Maybe I associate it too strongly with Satetsu Takeda. Iguchi is smart, though. Then Satetsu it is.

190
00:30:55,440 --> 00:31:06,800
What middle school are you from? Are you a thug? Hiyo, people will start suspecting you used to be in a girls' biker gang.

191
00:31:06,800 --> 00:31:17,620
What middle school? Normal people don't casually say that! “Jump up and down for me.” Now that's an actual delinquent.

192
00:31:17,620 --> 00:31:28,500
Why make someone jump? To check whether they have coins in their pockets. That's a sign of the times. Coins still exist, though.

193
00:31:28,500 --> 00:31:38,180
But now we're cashless. “Cashless”? Don't say it like someone from the Showa era who pronounces “zerii” as “jerii.”

194
00:31:38,180 --> 00:31:47,960
Or saying “mattsugu” instead of “massugu.” Some old-school Tokyo people still say that. They do.

195
00:31:47,960 --> 00:31:54,880
What would they say now that we're cashless? Instead of “Jump,” maybe, “Hey, say PayPay.” Send me some PayPay.

196
00:31:54,880 --> 00:32:06,640
You use PayPay? “Hey, bark ‘WAON’ for me.” Woof! Something like that, I guess.

197
00:32:06,640 --> 00:32:14,540
Let's make Iguchi “Pay”... Not Pay—PayPay. Shall we call him PayPay? Iguchi is PayPay now.

198
00:32:14,540 --> 00:32:23,940
He's the producer, after all. PayPay. P-P! Let's go with PP.

199
00:32:23,940 --> 00:32:38,940
Yama-chan can stay Yama-chan. This is my first time working with him on a radio show. We've worked on productions together, but I called him Yamazaki then.

200
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:53,160
You're giving everyone's names away! We're adopting Chanko's way of addressing people. What does Chanko call him? Yama-chan? Then Yama-chan it is.

201
00:32:54,160 --> 00:33:08,300
How do you feel about Koichi Yamadera, since you get called Yama-chan too? Hmm, he's thinking about it.

202
00:33:08,300 --> 00:33:23,940
Bazooka? Bazooka Yamazaki? What's that? Did Yamadera used to go by “Bazooka Yamadera”? When he was a radio DJ?

203
00:33:23,940 --> 00:33:35,940
I never knew that! Let's stick with Yama-chan. “Bazooka” would be far too roundabout.

204
00:33:35,940 --> 00:33:44,560
Our manager is “Liza Iizuka.” Liz Lisa? How nostalgic! So Heisei-era.

205
00:33:44,560 --> 00:33:56,020
There was a time when all the voice actresses wore Liz Lisa, wasn't there? And Franche Lippee. Amavel too.

206
00:33:56,020 --> 00:34:06,140
Everyone wore over-the-knee socks. But you didn't really have that phase, did you, Hiyo? I wore them. Did you?

207
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:17,700
I picture you wearing over-the-knee socks with shorts, not a frilly skirt. Right, I didn't really wear those. I was more of a shorts person.

208
00:34:17,700 --> 00:34:31,320
I wore shorts for Ro-Kyu-Bu! too. I performed at Animelo Summer Live recently, and someone sent me a video from ten years ago.

209
00:34:31,320 --> 00:34:41,040
The shorts I wore then were this short—almost like a high-cut swimsuit, or bloomers. Really?

210
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:52,480
You're definitely exaggerating. If you aren't, that's a pretty serious problem. Does that mean Hana wore those too?

211
00:34:53,200 --> 00:35:04,700
Everyone's bottoms were different. We each had our own lower half. Only Yoko had the high-cut ones? I was the one with really short shorts.

212
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:19,360
If you're exaggerating, Yoko... Careful! Like Yasumura. Anyway... You just said, “I'm not wearing any!”

213
00:35:19,360 --> 00:35:24,300
I'm not wearing any! You are wearing them! Oh, you were? I was!

214
00:35:24,300 --> 00:35:39,080
Were they really that short? Short ones were fashionable then. Even so, that's too much! Found it? Oh, you're right!

215
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:51,160
They really might have been. You can tell what era it's from, right? You have nice legs, Hiyo; they probably wanted to show them off.

216
00:35:51,160 --> 00:35:58,300
The things you do in the recklessness of your twenties! Weren't you about 26 here?

217
00:36:01,660 --> 00:36:13,820
It really shows the era... From a distance, that length really does look strange. It does.

218
00:36:13,820 --> 00:36:25,060
Those are briefs! They're practically the same size as briefs. Underpants!

219
00:36:25,060 --> 00:36:34,960
Did you wear them again this year? No, this year was different. We all wore the same style with different patterns.

220
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:45,080
That won't satisfy me anymore. Have you become impossible to satisfy after seeing those briefs? Nothing less than briefs will do now?

221
00:36:45,080 --> 00:36:52,220
Wow, they're really showing in this one. “I'm not wearing any!” Even from the other camera angles, it looks like you're not wearing anything.

222
00:36:52,220 --> 00:37:00,060
It's because the camera is filming from below. We're on a stage. Things really were different back then.

223
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:10,900
Over-the-knee socks and tall boots were fashionable then, so the shorts naturally got shorter. Long skirts are in fashion now.

224
00:37:10,900 --> 00:37:22,880
Depending on someone's legs, very short hemlines can make them look longer, while just above the knee makes other people's legs look slimmer. That's true.

225
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:34,760
I couldn't show that much now. Briefs are out of the question. Weren't briefs out of the question even then? Surely they were never okay.

226
00:37:34,760 --> 00:37:42,560
I don't picture you wearing short things much, Saku. I used to. Find it! Find Saku's briefs!

227
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:54,280
The outfit on my photobook cover was really short. The black, cherry-blossom-colored one, right? No. This kind. Yes, that part is right.

228
00:37:54,280 --> 00:38:06,680
You're making fun of me. But that photo really was beautiful. Oh, you're right. It's pretty sexy.

229
00:38:06,680 --> 00:38:17,640
My mother scolded me for this. Not for showing too much skin—for the red soles on the high heels.

230
00:38:17,640 --> 00:38:29,940
As usual, she picks something I don't understand. What do you mean? My mom, Shinobu, said, “High heels with red soles are indecent.”

231
00:38:29,940 --> 00:38:40,280
I was thinking, “Mom, there's something a little higher up that you ought to notice before the shoes.”

232
00:38:40,280 --> 00:38:51,380
Why would red heels be bad? Maybe they looked too grown-up. How old were you? About 23 or 24.

233
00:38:51,380 --> 00:39:03,920
Sexy! It's impressive to have that much allure at 23. You're making fun of me! It is beautiful, though.

234
00:39:03,920 --> 00:39:13,920
So get rid of the roses! They're red! They're indecent! I'll read a message.

235
00:39:13,920 --> 00:39:25,360
Saku, won't you release another photobook when you turn 30? I already wrote “first and last.” Just do “first and last” one more time.

236
00:39:25,360 --> 00:39:36,640
The first and last, the final chapter, then parts one and two of the final chapter's conclusion. Stop making fun of Attack on Titan! I was going to say the same thing...

237
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:43,880
I got told off for mentioning a Jump series earlier. I was about to say it but held back, so why did you bring it up?

238
00:39:43,880 --> 00:39:50,600
That isn't a spoiler. The Final Season, the conclusion, parts one and two. That's a spoiler! There are so many parts!

239
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:57,800
That's fine. The longer it goes on, the happier fans are. That's a spoiler! We're happy it's getting adapted to the end.

240
00:39:57,800 --> 00:40:05,740
I'll give it back. Why? We're reading it! Let's read just one message, from Aono Hanten. Poor thing, almost getting handed back unread.

241
00:40:05,740 --> 00:40:16,820
“Greetings, Hikasa and Sakura. Since you said it was okay to change Sakura's Wikipedia photo to the Ao Oni promotional image, I decided to try editing Wikipedia for the first time and went to the page.”

242
00:40:16,820 --> 00:40:23,580
“It said the page is protected to prevent vandalism, so I didn't have the power to edit it.” What does that mean?

243
00:40:23,700 --> 00:40:32,420
“Conversely, that means someone with the power to edit Wikipedia chose that picture of Sakura with the roses in the background.”

244
00:40:32,720 --> 00:40:42,220
“First, I think we need to put out a wanted notice to find someone with Wikipedia editing rights. That concludes my report.”

245
00:40:42,220 --> 00:40:51,100
Hiyo, what were you distracted by while reading? I thought something was flickering over here. Didn't something flash just now? That's scary.

246
00:40:51,100 --> 00:41:00,860
Don't act like a cat staring at something we can't see! That's scary. Maybe you should go to a hospital. Stop it! Don't send me there!

247
00:41:00,860 --> 00:41:11,220
What is this? What does it mean? Who has editing rights? Maybe people who pay money or something?

248
00:41:11,220 --> 00:41:23,160
Someone, pay Wikipedia and edit it, please! Who has that power? Surely someone in the Sakura fan brigade has editing rights?

249
00:41:23,160 --> 00:41:32,160
Maybe nobody does, given how much we've asked. It might be Elon Musk. Seriously? That's way too much power.

250
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:44,160
“This is the right photo for Sakura, because red is sexy.” Elon said that? Red is sexy! “The red is very XX,” he'd say.

251
00:41:44,160 --> 00:41:55,560
The one who said red was sexy was my mother. So your mother is Elon Musk? Our family conspiracy...

252
00:41:55,560 --> 00:42:10,600
Who is it, seriously? Please! If it hasn't changed by next week, what do we do? This show will be over!

253
00:42:10,600 --> 00:42:23,200
Is that okay? We've only done two episodes! Please, by next time. We're really asking you. Please do it.

254
00:42:23,200 --> 00:42:32,200
Is this going on YouTube? The picture quality won't be better there, will it? It'll be okay, right? It will be a little better.

255
00:42:32,200 --> 00:42:36,900
Better than Bunka Hoso's crappy videos. Not crappy videos—crappy picture quality.

256
00:42:36,900 --> 00:42:46,840
I've just realized that, so lower the quality for this episode only. I'm not wearing makeup. Just a little, just this time.

257
00:42:46,840 --> 00:42:55,100
But your face looks bright and clear. No, I'm having them lower it. Starting next week, we'll be in relatively high definition.

258
00:42:55,100 --> 00:43:03,180
That's all for the after-talk. Please enjoy the main episode too. Your hosts were Yoko Hikasa and Ayane Sakura. Bye-bye!

